{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792040000000-reviewer-srvm-phase6a-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md collab/1792030000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase6a-r3.report.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792040000000-reviewer-srvm-phase6a-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md collab/1792030000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase6a-r3.report.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-11 17:20:30 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-12 00:20:30 Asia/Bangkok\n\n# Adversarial review of slice 6a, round 3 (final sign-off pass)\n\nReviewer: claude-fable-5-1. Round-3 author: codex gpt-6.1-sol. Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a`, branch `srvm-phase6a`, HEAD 6b7e65ed; round-3 diff `git diff bb4a6558 HEAD` = `rows.cljc` (+123/−42 lines) and `rows_test.cljc` (+58/−1) only. Nothing staged or committed by me; at the end `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src test` exits 0 and both files `cmp` equal to `collab/6a-r3-reviewer-backups/{rows,rows_test}.cljc` (taken and verified before any mutant). The clock in this sandbox reads 17:19 GMT at completion, which is earlier than the brief's Created-GMT marker; the timestamps above are the actual clock.\n\n## What I ran (all JVM, foreground, `-J-Xmx256m`, through `python3 -I collab/6a-r3-reviewer-run.py` because the sandbox refuses a direct `clojure -J…` call)\n\n| Lane | Command | Result | Log |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline at 256 MiB | `clojure -J-Xmx256m -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test` | 30 tests, 549 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors, 11.7 s wall (includes the seven-cell refusal and the three pinned roots) | `collab/6a-r3-reviewer-baseline.log` |\n| Probe A (temporary `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_r3probe_test.cljc`, deleted afterwards) | `clojure -J-Xmx256m -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-r3probe-test` | 31 printed probes, see below | `collab/6a-r3-reviewer-probe.log`, `…-probe2.log` |\n| Probe B (temporary `rows_r3heavy_test.cljc`, deleted) | `clojure -J-Xmx256m -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-r3heavy-test` | 9 timed symmetric-cell cases | `collab/6a-r3-reviewer-heavy.log` |\n| Mutation campaign | `python3 -I collab/6a-r3-reviewer-mutants.py`, each mutant `clojure -J-Xmx256m -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -v yin.vm.ucf.rows-test/<test>` | 15 mutants: 11 KILLED, 4 SURVIVED; every run restored from the verified backup, `cmp` 0 and `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` 0 | `collab/6a-r3-reviewer-X01.log` … `X15.log`, `collab/6a-r3-reviewer-mutants.json` |\n| Node, Dart, kondo, cljstyle | **not run by me.** The author's `collab/6a-r3-node-final.log` shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`, 200 tests / 4828 assertions / 0 failures; the orchestrator reports kondo 0/0; Dart is the orchestrator's lane and has no result yet | — |\n\n## Findings (severity | file:line | evidence | correction)\n\n1. **MEDIUM (new in round 3) | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:351` | the search leaf's key puts the whole root row body under `:label-bytes`.** `search` computes the leaf key as `(checked-key (checked-encode (subvec (get rows id) 1)))`; the root task row body carries the inline `:D` slots (profile, status, custody slot 10, the code map slot 11, slots 14 and 16) and the `:optional-V` result literal in slot 15. These are the input's own bytes, not an intermediate label, yet they are now refused at 1 MiB whenever the task has at least one cell. Probe (both runs): `(rows/emit (task-node [[:x (cell-body :s 0)]] [:lit (vec (range 400000))]))` → `{:status :yin.k/unsupported :kind :resource-limit :path [:label-bytes]}`, while the identical task with **no** cell emits (nil), and the same literal in a `:vec` row or a `:store` row emits and decodes. Custody variant `(assoc slots 16 (vec (range 400000)))` with one cell → the same refusal. Round 2 (bb4a6558) had no byte bound on emit, so this is an accepted-domain regression; decode is affected too, because `decode*` re-runs `emit` for `:noncanonical-cells`, so a 1.5 MiB wire that the 16 MiB `:bytes` budget admits becomes undecodable when it has a cell. Correction (one line, no canonical-byte change): the leaf key is the digest preimage of the root body, bounded already by the task itself (and by `:bytes` on decode); compute it with plain `cbor/encode`/`bytes-key`, or bound it with `(:bytes budget)` instead of `(:label-bytes budget)`. Add a test: one cell plus a ~1.2 MiB inline result literal emits and decodes.\n\n2. **MEDIUM (pre-existing, surfaced by brief question 3) | `rows.cljc:205-209, 259, 290, 326-330` | a `:work` unit costs time proportional to the current label size, so the work bound does not bound time.** Under default budgets at 256 MiB: 7 equal-body cells in one `:set` → `:resource-limit [:work]` after **44 s**; 7 equal-body cells as `:map` keys with distinct literal values → `[:work]` after **35 s**; 10 such map keys → `[:work]` after **748 s** (12.5 minutes) for a wire of a few hundred bytes; 20 and 64 map keys refuse `[:label-bytes]` in 2.4 s and 9.6 s. (The map case never refines because draft §6 step 3 gives a key the position `[kind :key CE(k)]`, which carries no information about its value, so the search must individualize; that is the design as written.) `check-label-bytes!` bounds each label, but `classes`, `refine` and `checked-sort` then compare labels via `bytes-key`, an O(label) boxed vector per comparison, charged one `:work` unit regardless of size. Not introduced in round 3 (rounds 1-2 had the same `:work` semantics), and §10.10 leaves elapsed time optional, so this is not a blocker for this slice; it should be fixed before 6b exposes `decode` to peers. Correction within §10.10's latitude (no canonical-byte change): charge `:work` in proportion to bytes keyed/compared (e.g. `(quot (alength bs) 1024)` per `checked-key`), or add a cumulative `:label-bytes-total` budget.\n\n3. **LOW | `rows.cljc:614-619` vs `608-611` | default decode still produces in-band markers that default encode refuses.** `decoded-value` returns `{:yin.k/row n}` / `{:yin.k/cell id}`; `value-node` now refuses exactly those shapes. Probe: encode a frame with the explicit test encoder, `rows->frame` with the default decoder, then `frame->rows` with the default encoder → `:yin.k/non-portable :marker`. So `frame->rows ∘ rows->frame` is identity only when both sides are given a classifier. The decode direction is unambiguous (guest shapes are refused on the wire), so this is a usability asymmetry, not a soundness hole. Correction: say so in the `rows->frame` docstring, or make the default decoder return `[:row n]`/`[:cell id]` tagged vectors that the default encoder accepts.\n\n4. **LOW | `rows.cljc:91-94, 326-330` | the bound is on CBOR bytes, the allocation is ~8× larger and held per cell.** `bytes-key` boxes every byte; `classes` holds one such vector per cell in its reduce map. Worst case under defaults is 64 cells × 1 MiB × ~8 ≈ 0.5 GiB live, which fits a default JVM heap but not `-Xmx256m`. Empirically the 64-key map case refused cleanly at 256 MiB (labels double per round and cross the bound at ~0.5 MiB), and I found no input that reached OOM; the exposure is theoretical. Correction (optional, no canonical-byte change): compare byte arrays directly instead of boxing (note `cbor/encoded-compare` is length-first and cannot be substituted as is).\n\n5. **INFO | mutants X08-X10, X13, X15 survive because they remove redundant layers.** Removing the preflight from `checked-encode` alone, from `checked-key` alone, or the `doseq` in `checked-sort` alone still refuses the seven-cell fixture through the remaining site (X08's survival means the refusal then comes *after* one unbounded `cbor/encode`, bounded in practice by the preceding round's checked label, so no OOM at 256 MiB). The decode-side marker loop (X13, disclosed by the author) and `decoded-value`'s marker refusal (X15) are redundant with the emit-side check that re-emission applies. None is a defect; each is defence in depth. Removing *all* refusal (X11) and the combined emit removal (X14) are killed.\n\n6. **INFO | `rows.cljc:164-166, 546-550` | the marker refusal reaches every `:D` slot, including carried code and stream descriptors.** Probes: a code-map constant `[:const 1 {:yin.k/row 1}]` and a stream descriptor `{:yin.k/row 1}` are refused `:non-portable`. This is what \"every literal context\" means and `:yin.k` is a reserved namespace, but note that a guest program whose code literally contains such a map is now non-portable where round 2 carried it.\n\n7. **INFO | `rows.cljc:226` | numbers are sized at a flat 128 bytes.** A 60 000-digit bigint in a stream descriptor is under-counted (probe completed without error). Label growth is multiplicative, so the estimate stays proportional and the bound still terminates; no action needed.\n\n## Answers\n\n**1. The four round-2 findings.** All closed. (a) Marker-shaped guest maps are refused in every literal context on encode and decode: besides the author's twenty assertions (task result, nested vector, symbol metadata, store value, custody; wire forgeries of each), my probes refuse them in a stream descriptor, nested inside a map value, inside a set literal, as a map key, in vector metadata, in `:bindings` via `frame->rows`, in a `:put` pending value via `frame->rows`, and in a code-map constant. Well-formed markers (`{:yin.k/row (node :prim …)}`) are refused regardless of payload validity (X01 killed). (b) The explicit path round-trips: `bijection` on all reference frames with the test encoder, and the well-formed guest map round-trips under the explicit encoder. (c) No previously legitimate value is refused: two-key maps `{:yin.k/row 1 :yin.k/cell 2}`, the empty map, nil-valued envelopes still round-trip; the only accepted-domain change I found is finding 1, which is the byte bound, not the marker rule. (d) Task-result literals and the `[:identity …]` false duplicate are fixed (X06 killed).\n\n**2. Tagged equality.** Injective by construction: every guest literal is wrapped (`[:literal x]`, `[:seq …]`, `[:set …]`, `[:map …]`, recursively), cells are `[:identity id]`, opaque rows `[:row n]`; a guest value can never spell a bare `[:identity …]` or `[:row …]` because its vectors become `[:seq …]`. Probes: cell vs `[:row {:kind :prim …}]` literal, `:prim` row vs row-shaped literal, `:map` row vs `[:map {…}]`-shaped literal, `:set` row vs `#{[:identity …]}` literal all decode (nil). Genuine duplicates preserved: two equal `:map` rows, `:vec` vs `:list` rows with equal contents, and `[x [1]]` vs `[x (1)]` all refuse `:duplicate-key` (X07 killed by `schema-and-literal-canonicality`). Canonical bytes unmoved: `canonical-roots-are-pinned` passes at HEAD with the three pins (`…be82d50b…` for `(fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y)`, `…c4a7c7e6…` for `(frame->rows ft/worked-frame)`, `…8899f95d…` for the three-cell pin), and X12 shows the test does check them (killed, 2 errors). `checked-sort` keeps `sorted-bytes`' comparator; nothing on the emission path changed a byte.\n\n**3. The `:label-bytes` bound.** The preflight precedes allocation at every intermediate site: initial labels, position encodings, signatures, individualization labels, `checked-sort` comparisons, class keys and the leaf key all go through `checked-encode`/`checked-key`, which size the Clojure value (byte payloads by native length, no copy) before `cbor/encode` or `bytes-key`. The seven-cell fixture refuses at 256 MiB inside the 11.7 s baseline run, as does the whole suite. The one path that still allocates more than the bound suggests is `bytes-key`'s boxing (finding 4), bounded but ~8× and per cell; I found no unbounded path. Realistic sizes: 10, 20 and 64 equal-body cells in a `:vec` canonicalize in ≤ 20 ms; 4-6 equal-body cells in a `:set` or as `:map` keys canonicalize (0.1-3.8 s); two mutual pairs (4 cells) canonicalize; **three mutual pairs (6 cells) refuse `[:label-bytes]`** in 0.3 s, and 7+ equal-body cells in a set or map refuse `[:work]` slowly (finding 2). The default therefore spares ordered and refinement-separable tasks of any size but refuses symmetric structures from six equal-body cells; whether six cursors on one stream at one position arranged in mutual pairs is \"realistic\" is the Architect's call. The refusal kind `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:label-bytes]` matches `spend!` exactly. Finding 1 is the one place the bound is applied to the wrong quantity. **Design amendment:** none is *required*. §10.10 adopts a work bound with implementation-chosen limits that must not alter canonical bytes; refusing the seven-cell case is compliant, and the fixture is not a canonicality test. Two things should be recorded before 6b: (i) §10.10's list (bytes, rows, cells, depth, refinement work, search nodes) should name label/signature bytes as a bounded resource and require `:work` to be charged in proportion to bytes processed; (ii) the open question whether the §6 labels should be hashed per round (constant size, no growth bound needed) must be decided knowing it **changes canonical bytes** (class order is bytewise by label) and re-pins all three roots, so it is an amendment, not a fix. The current refusal is the conservative choice.\n\n**4. Mutants.** Fifteen applied, table below; eleven killed by the named test, four survivors explained in finding 5.\n\n**5. Scope and portability.** `git diff bb4a6558 HEAD --stat` lists exactly `rows.cljc` and `rows_test.cljc`; `frame.cljc` untouched. Every `assoc` in the diff is single-key or on a map (`(assoc (:slots …) 6 …)`, `(assoc (subvec row 1) 16 …)`, `(assoc ls id …)`); no `for`; no `#'` in source or test. The only reader conditional is `#?(:cljd (.-length x) :default (alength x))` with `:cljd` first. There is **no** test that spawns a subprocess or sets a heap: `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` runs `canonical-numbering` in-process under default budgets and merely *was run* under `-J-Xmx256m` by the author; it is therefore portable as written and the author's Node log shows it green. Dart remains unrun (orchestrator's lane); the Dart-specific branch is `.-length` on a `Uint8List`, which is correct by inspection.\n\n## Mutant table\n\n| ID | Mutation (rows.cljc) | Named test | Result | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| X01 | `value-node` back to round-2 shape-based classification | `well-formed-guest-markers-are-refused` | KILLED (3 assertions, 2 failures) | guest/explicit distinction |\n| X02 | skip marker check for `:store` rows on encode and decode | `every-literal-context-refuses-markers` | KILLED (20, 4 failures) | store values |\n| X03 | drop the metadata recursion | same | KILLED (20, 4 failures) | metadata |\n| X04 | drop the nested-collection recursion | same | KILLED (20, 4 failures) | nested vectors |\n| X05 | skip `:optional-D` slots on encode and decode | same | KILLED (20, 4 failures) | custody |\n| X06 | guest literals untagged (`:lit (second v)`) | `cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct` | KILLED (2, 1 failure 1 error) | equality tags collapsed |\n| X07 | literal vector and literal list tagged differently | `schema-and-literal-canonicality` | KILLED (12, 1 failure) | genuine duplicate preserved |\n| X08 | preflight removed from `checked-encode` | `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` | SURVIVED (2, 0) | redundant with `checked-key` |\n| X09 | preflight removed from `checked-key` | same | SURVIVED (2, 0) | redundant with `checked-encode` |\n| X10 | `doseq` preflight removed from `checked-sort` | same | SURVIVED (2, 0) | redundant with the comparator's checks |\n| X11 | `check-label-bytes!` never refuses | same | KILLED (2, 2 failures) | all sites |\n| X12 | default `:label-bytes 1` | `canonical-roots-are-pinned` | KILLED (3, 2 errors) | |\n| X13 | decode-side marker loop removed only | `every-literal-context-refuses-markers` | SURVIVED (20, 0) | author-disclosed; re-emission refuses |\n| X14 | emit-side marker check removed only | same | KILLED (20, 10 failures) | |\n| X15 | `decoded-value` marker refusal removed | `marker-shaped-guest-maps-are-not-portable` | SURVIVED (5, 0) | redundant with decode loop |\n\nEvery mutant: source `cmp`-equal to the verified backup before, restored from it after, `cmp` 0 and `git diff --quiet HEAD -- src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` 0; no OOM in any log.\n\n## Required before merge\n\nFinding 1 only: take the leaf key out of `:label-bytes` (plain encode, or bound by `:bytes`) and add the one-cell large-root-literal test; re-run the exact JVM frame+rows command and Node. Findings 2-4 can follow in a separate slice; finding 2 should land before 6b exposes `decode` to untrusted peers. Everything else in round 3 is confirmed.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-11 16:51:10 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-11 23:51:10 Asia/Bangkok\n\n\n# Slice 6a round 3\n\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6a`, branch `srvm-phase6a`, HEAD `bb4a6558`. Only `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` changed. `frame.cljc` is untouched. Nothing staged or committed. The completion timestamp uses the actual clock; it is earlier than the brief's Created-GMT marker.\n\n## Changes and regression evidence\n\nThe four final named tests were run against the original source restored from the pre-edit backup, with a 256 MiB JVM heap and a 90-second subprocess timeout. Result: **4 tests, 27 assertions, 25 failures, 1 error**, exit 1 (`collab/6a-r3-pre-final-tests.log`). The fixed source was restored byte for byte. The same four tests then passed on final bytes: **4 tests, 27 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`collab/6a-r3-regressions-final.log`).\n\n| Item | Change and evidence | Named regression, old-source result | Final result | Required mutant on final source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 MEDIUM | **SOURCE:** default `value-node` classifies guest values as literals and refuses marker-shaped maps regardless of payload validity. Explicit references come from the caller's encoder argument as `[:row node]` / `[:cell id]`. **TEST:** the well-formed `{:yin.k/row (rows/node :prim ['guest/f])}` window probe and keyword-cell variant both refuse; explicit classification still round-trips. Existing portable fixtures now supply their classifier explicitly, including captured frames. | `well-formed-guest-markers-are-refused`: 1 test / 3 assertions, 2 failures | 3 assertions pass in final regression run | `guest`: restore shape-based interpretation in the default classifier; **KILLED**, 1 test / 3 assertions, 2 failures, 0 errors |\n| 2 MEDIUM | **SOURCE:** recursive marker refusal in every plain/literal slot during emission and wire decoding, using slot descriptors, including task results, store values, custody, nested collections, and symbol/collection metadata. Structural references and explicit value references are separate. **TEST:** both review maps `{:yin.k/row 1}` / `{:yin.k/cell :c}` are tried on encode; independently rehashed wire probes verify decode without depending on encode accepting the invalid literal. | `every-literal-context-refuses-markers`: 1 test / 20 assertions, 20 failures | 20 assertions pass in final regression run | `literal-both`: remove literal checking from emission and decoding; **KILLED**, 1 test / 20 assertions, 20 failures, 0 errors |\n| 3 MEDIUM | **SOURCE:** decoded equality uses recursive tagged representations: literals, sequences, maps, sets, identities and opaque reference rows have disjoint forms. Guest collections are recursively tagged, so a cell cannot collide with a guest vector that spells its internal tag. Vector/list equality remains shared, preserving the existing genuine duplicate refusal. **TEST:** cell `:x` and the distinct literal `[:identity :yin.k/c-1]` coexist in one set; decode and decode/re-emit both succeed. | `cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`: 1 test / 2 assertions, 1 failure, 1 error (`:duplicate-key`) | 2 assertions pass in final regression run; existing duplicate-key tests also pass in G0 | `equality`: return untagged guest literals; **KILLED**, 1 test / 2 assertions, 1 failure, 1 error |\n| 4 HIGH | **SOURCE:** add default `:label-bytes 1048576` and preflight intermediate encodings and unsigned byte comparison vectors before CBOR encoding or comparison-vector allocation. This covers initial labels, position encodings, signatures, individualization labels, unordered CE comparisons, class keys, and leaf comparison keys. The preflight counts existing byte payload lengths without copying and includes text/container/metadata overhead. Native byte-array length is used on all hosts. Refusal is status `:yin.k/unsupported`, kind `:resource-limit`, path/reason `[:label-bytes]`, consistent with existing local budget refusals. **TEST:** the equal-body seven-cell directed triangle/square fixture refuses under default budgets; its regression is run at `-J-Xmx256m`. | `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded`: 1 test / 2 assertions, 2 failures (caught Throwable yielded no ex-data; its class was not recorded by the helper) | 2 assertions pass; separate final bounded-heap run: 1 test / 2 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors | `bytes`: remove preflight's size check; **KILLED**, 1 test / 2 assertions, 2 failures, 0 errors; 256 MiB heap and 90-second timeout |\n| 5 LOW | No source change. Install-child emission still has no caller `budget` in scope: `emit-node` takes `[n numbering rows]`. Passing it requires threading through emission helpers/callers, rather than the conditional one-token change authorized by this item. | Not changed; no new test or mutant claimed | Existing child tests pass in final suites | Not applicable |\n\n**Whether the seven-cell fixture should instead canonicalize remains an open Architect question. A hashed-label formulation would change the numbering; this round does not decide it.** The fix preserves the label formulation and accepted-fixture bytes, while refusing this resource-intensive fixture.\n\nAll three pinned roots remain unchanged and pass `canonical-roots-are-pinned` on JVM and Node:\n\n- fixture: `:segment/blake3-be82d50b14804bb7a69103631b9c45759b49b67dbb3cebe75a1259fed6b4a937`\n- worked frame: `:segment/blake3-c4a7c7e6de3b44400efec5930bc3d85d86b29153b4b2d548acba630bfbc3da7d`\n- three-cell loop/two-cycle: `:segment/blake3-8899f95d330a174ee098e10f16ecf2d017d5aae67d4f50d8369222342e6d36e9`\n\nThe ten-cell census case also still succeeds and round-trips.\n\n## Mutations and restoration\n\nBefore any edit, source bytes were copied to `collab/6a-r3-backups/pre/rows.cljc` and equality verified. Before mutations, fixed source bytes were copied to `collab/6a-r3-backups/rows.cljc` and equality verified. Later source corrections each had an already verified matching backup and additional snapshots (`rows-before-byte-length.cljc`, `rows-before-symbol-meta.cljc`); the final mutation backup was refreshed and verified before reapplying all four required mutants. Each mutation starts by checking source equality, restores from the verified backup in `finally`, and verifies exact byte equality after restoration. The old-source regression run uses the same restoration discipline.\n\nFinal-source mutation commands are literal, foreground subprocesses, each with `-J-Xmx256m` and a 90-second timeout:\n\n```sh\nclojure -J-Xmx256m -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -v yin.vm.ucf.rows-test/well-formed-guest-markers-are-refused\nclojure -J-Xmx256m -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -v yin.vm.ucf.rows-test/every-literal-context-refuses-markers\nclojure -J-Xmx256m -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -v yin.vm.ucf.rows-test/cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct\nclojure -J-Xmx256m -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -v yin.vm.ucf.rows-test/intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded\n```\n\nHarness and final records: `collab/6a-r3-mutants.py`, `collab/6a-r3-mutants.json`; logs `6a-r3-guest.log`, `6a-r3-literal-both.log`, `6a-r3-equality.log`, `6a-r3-bytes.log`.\n\nAdditional mutation attempts, stated plainly:\n\n- Dropping only emission-side literal checking was killed: 1 test / 16 assertions, 8 failures (`6a-r3-literal-encode.log`, before metadata assertions were added).\n- Dropping only decoding-side checking **SURVIVED**: 1 test / 16 assertions, 0 failures/errors (`6a-r3-literal-decode.log`). Canonical re-emission applies the emission-side check, so the same refusal still occurs. The combined removal above is the required killed literal-context mutant. No claim that the decode-only mutant was killed.\n- Initial new tests had two faulty decode probes that forged `[:lit nil]` instead of the intended marker. Those probes were corrected before the final old-source regression and final mutation campaign. Earlier scratch logs are not final verification.\n- First Node run exposed my use of `count` on `Uint8Array`: 200 tests / 4773 assertions, 4 failures, 16 errors (`6a-r3-node.log`; build process exited 0 despite test failures). Replaced it with native array length and reran mutations and lanes; final results below are green.\n\nFinal source SHA-256 and backup SHA-256 both equal `eaad67ef85c01bcb151273fea8b053571a790fb15aac63938b24bc58d5995355`. Pre-edit source SHA-256 is `f5f5ee8525f91075ee4abbf68bf760ec35d2df20def6371816b22a28db7fd637`. Recorded in `collab/6a-r3-backups/verification.json`. `git diff --check` is clean; staged diff is empty.\n\n## Final lanes (foreground; no slow tests)\n\n| Lane | Exact counts / result | Log |\n|---|---|---|\n| Exact requested JVM frame + rows command | **30 tests, 549 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, exit 0 | `collab/6a-r3-g0-final.log` |\n| JVM 14 namespaces, round-1 set | **195 tests, 4769 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, exit 0 | `collab/6a-r3-jvm-final.log` |\n| JVM parity dependency | **5 tests, 60 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, exit 0; unaffected by subsequent byte-length/metadata corrections | `collab/6a-r3-jvm-parity.log` |\n| Node, same selection as round 2, includes frame-test and rows-test | **200 tests, 4828 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**; build exit 0, 0 warnings | `collab/6a-r3-node-final.log` |\n| Final four regressions, 256 MiB heap | **4 tests, 27 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, exit 0 | `collab/6a-r3-regressions-final.log` |\n| Final seven-cell default-budget probe, 256 MiB heap | **1 test, 2 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, exit 0 | `collab/6a-r3-byte-bound-final.log` |\n| kondo, both changed files | **0 errors, 0 warnings**, exit 0 | `collab/6a-r3-kondo-final.log` |\n| cljstyle, both changed files | Clean, exit 0; mise emitted a cache-write warning outside the workspace | `collab/6a-r3-cljstyle-final.log` |\n| Dart | **NOT RUN by me**; orchestrator runs this lane | No Dart result claimed |\n\nCommands:\n\n```sh\nclojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test\nclojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.code-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.analysis-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.linearize-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.vm-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.effects-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.parity-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.link-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.load-paths-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.walker-trace-test -n yin.vm.ucf-test -n yin.vm.ucf.handoff-test -n yin.vm.ucf.safepoint-harness-test\nclojure -M:test -n yin.vm.parity-test\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test --config-merge '{:ns-regexp \"^(yin\\\\.vm\\\\.semantic-register\\\\..*-test|yin\\\\.vm\\\\.ucf\\\\.(rows|handoff|safepoint-harness)-test|yin\\\\.vm\\\\.ucf-test)$\"}'\nclj -M:kondo --lint src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\nmise exec -- cljstyle check src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\n```\n\nThe bounded regression commands use the four literal named tests listed in the mutation section; the final regression run supplies all four `-v` arguments in one command. All final source and test bytes were stable during the final G0, 14-namespace JVM, Node, bounded regression, kondo and cljstyle runs.\n\nClojureDart inspection: searched every `assoc` in both touched files. All vector associations are single-key; remaining multi-key associations operate on established maps. No multi-key `assoc` on a vector or nil was introduced or found in these files. This inspection does not substitute for the orchestrator's Dart run.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
